Authorization
Other applicable constraints
The architectural preference is:
Local when sufficient.
Remote when necessary and authorized.
Fallback when appropriate.
Failure when neither is acceptable.
PRIVACY
Core AI must treat privacy as a first-class architectural constraint.
Core AI must not assume that information may be transmitted simply because a model can process it.
Before information reaches an intelligence provider, the applicable architecture must determine:
Whether transmission is permitted.
Which information is necessary.
Which provider is authorized.
Which context is necessary.
Whether local processing is sufficient.
Whether sensitive information must be minimized.
Whether the operation requires explicit authorization.
Core AI must support privacy-preserving processing patterns.
Where feasible:
Minimize context.
Minimize exposure.
Prefer local processing.
Use authorized providers.
Avoid unnecessary retention.
Avoid unnecessary logging.
Avoid unnecessary replication.
Privacy is not a secondary feature of Core AI.
Privacy is part of the conditions under which intelligence is allowed to operate.
ACCESSIBILITY
Core AI must support accessibility as an architectural requirement.
Accessibility is not limited to the presentation layer.
