Alternative communication support
Context-aware completion
Other accessibility capabilities
The underlying Personal Intelligence must remain the same.
Accessibility changes how intelligence is expressed and accessed.
It does not create a lesser intelligence environment.
HUMAN AGENCY
Core AI must support human agency.
Intelligence generation does not equal authorization to act.
A model may recommend an action.
That does not mean the action is authorized.
A model may generate an instruction.
That does not mean the instruction should be executed.
A model may identify a possible operation.
That does not mean the operation is permitted.
Execution authority remains governed by the applicable system, privacy, security, authorization, and automation contracts.
Core AI must therefore distinguish:
Suggestion
Decision support
Generation
Planning
Execution
The existence of intelligence does not automatically imply execution authority.
TRUTHFULNESS
Core AI must preserve truthful representation of its capabilities and operations.
It must not claim:
A tool was used when it was not.
A source was consulted when it was not.
An action was executed when it was not.
A provider responded when it did not.
A file was created when it was not.
A memory was stored when it was not.
A capability was available when it was unavailable.
A result was verified when it was not.
The system must preserve the distinction between:
Generated
Retrieved
Observed
Executed
Verified
Inferred
Requested
Completed
Failed
This distinction is essential to trust.
